<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
<title>Limits, Partial Derivatives, and Optimization</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Show all work and justify your conclusions. Explain geometric meanings whenever appropriate.

<url href="https://opentext.uleth.ca/apex-calculus/chapter_multi.html"/>
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<title>A Limit That Does Not Exist</title>

<p>
Consider
<m>
f(x,y)=\frac{x^2y}{x^4+y^2}.
</m>
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>
Evaluate
<m>
\lim_{x\to 0}f(x,0).
</m>
</li>

<li>
Evaluate
<m>
\lim_{y\to 0}f(0,y).
</m>
</li>

<li>
Based on parts (a) and (b), what might you expect for
<m>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)}f(x,y)?
</m>
</li>

<li>
Investigate the limit along
<m>
y=x^2.
</m>

<p>
<em>Hint:</em> Substitute <m>y=x^2</m> into the formula.
</p>
</li>

<li>
Determine whether
<m>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)}f(x,y)
</m>
exists.
</li>

<li>
Explain why checking only the coordinate axes is not sufficient.
</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<title>First and Second Partial Derivatives</title>

<p>
Let
<m>
f(x,y)=x^3y^2+2x^2y-y^3.
</m>
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>Find <m>f_x</m>.</li>
<li>Find <m>f_y</m>.</li>
<li>Find <m>f_{xx}</m>.</li>
<li>Find <m>f_{yy}</m>.</li>
<li>Find <m>f_{xy}</m>.</li>
<li>Find <m>f_{yx}</m>.</li>
<li>Compare <m>f_{xy}</m> and <m>f_{yx}</m>.</li>
<li>State a theorem that explains your observation.</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<title>Directional Derivative and Elevation</title>

<p>
Let
<m>
f(x,y)=x^2+xy+y^2.
</m>
</p>

<p>
Consider the point <m>(1,1)</m>.
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>Find <m>\nabla f(x,y)</m>.</li>

<li>Evaluate <m>\nabla f(1,1)</m>.</li>

<li>Convert <m>(1,2)</m> to a unit vector.</li>

<li>
Find the directional derivative at <m>(1,1)</m>
in the direction <m>(1,2)</m>.
</li>

<li>
Interpret the directional derivative geometrically.
</li>

<li>
What does your answer say about the elevation of the surface
as you move in the direction <m>(1,2)</m>?
</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<title>Critical Point and Hessian: Local Maximum</title>

<p>
Consider
<m>
f(x,y)=10-x^2-y^2.
</m>
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>Find all first partial derivatives.</li>

<li>Find all critical points.</li>

<li>
Compute the Hessian matrix
<m>
H=
\begin{bmatrix}
f_{xx} &amp; f_{xy}\\
f_{yx} &amp; f_{yy}
\end{bmatrix}.
</m>
</li>

<li>Evaluate the Hessian at each critical point.</li>

<li>Use the Second Derivative Test.</li>

<li>Classify the critical point.</li>

<li>
Graph the surface in GeoGebra 3D.
</li>

<li>
Describe the shape near the critical point and explain why
it is a maximum.
</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise>
<title>Critical Point and Hessian: Local Minimum</title>

<p>
Consider
<m>
f(x,y)=x^2+y^2+2.
</m>
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>Find all first partial derivatives.</li>

<li>Find all critical points.</li>

<li>Compute the Hessian matrix.</li>

<li>Evaluate the Hessian at each critical point.</li>

<li>Use the Second Derivative Test.</li>

<li>Classify the critical point.</li>

<li>Graph the surface in GeoGebra 3D.</li>

<li>
Describe the shape near the critical point and explain why
it is a minimum.
</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

</handout>
